---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LeadForm from "../components/LeadForm.astro";
import CallLink from "../components/CallLink.astro";

const title = "Water damage restoration in Loganville, GA";
const description =
  "Burst pipe, leak, basement water, or storm water in Loganville, GA 30052. Call or send what happened.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="hero wrap">
    <p class="muted">Gwinnett / Walton</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Water where it doesn't belong? We're here to help. Call or click "Request
      help in Loganville" now and fill out the form. Share as much as you can
      about the problem and we will get back to you as soon as we can.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="#request">Request help in Loganville</a>
      <CallLink class="btn secondary" />
      <a class="btn secondary" href="/what-to-do">First-hour checklist</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Water damage in Loganville</h2>
    <p>
      Loganville has one ZIP code, 30052, and two counties. The city sits on the
      line between Gwinnett and Walton. Two neighbors with the same mailing
      address can be in different counties. In town, U.S. 78 is Atlanta Highway.
      Rosebud and the border with Grayson are on the Gwinnett side.
    </p>
    <p>
      Many houses here have a basement or a terrace level, which is a finished
      level partly below ground. Water from outside collects there, and a leak
      upstairs can end up there too. That shows up more here than on a street of
      slab houses.
    </p>
    <p>
      Houses from the 1980s to the mid-1990s may have gray plastic pipe, called
      polybutylene, which is known to fail. The newer ones, in Rosebud and in the
      2000s subdivisions, are past that. In those, the water heater and the hoses
      are the usual trouble. You cannot tell from the neighborhood, so ask a
      plumber if you are not sure.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why the first questions are about your house. When you call or send
      the form, tell us where the water came from, which rooms are wet, and how
      long it has been there. Tell us if the house has a basement, a terrace
      level, a crawl space, or a slab. Tell us your street, and which county you
      think you are in.
    </p>
    <p>
      Storms do some damage. During Hurricane Helene in 2024, power lines came
      down on Loganville Highway, and on the Walton side there was 6 to 7 inches
      of rain, with trees down and one on a house. A damaged roof can leak into
      the attic for days before the stain shows up on the ceiling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Where people say they live</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Downtown and U.S. 78.</strong> The older core and the Atlanta
      Highway strip. Older houses here are more likely to sit over a crawl space.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Rosebud.</strong> On the Gwinnett side. Houses from the 1990s and
      2000s.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Bay Creek and Sharon.</strong> Established neighborhoods on the
      Walton side.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Youth.</strong> More rural, with larger lots. More septic, and more
      crawl spaces.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Ozora.</strong> Newer houses, on the line with Grayson.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Westgate Estates.</strong> New builder houses from the 2020s.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Common causes in Loganville</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Basement or terrace-level water.</strong> Water from outside, or a
        leak upstairs, ends up on the lower level. A cleanup there takes longer
        than a one-room leak.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Water heater.</strong> The tank leaks or lets go in a garage,
        closet, or basement.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Refrigerator or washer line.</strong> The hose cracks. Water slides
        under the floor, and you smell it before you see it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Gray plastic pipe.</strong> Some houses from the 1980s to the
        mid-1990s may have polybutylene pipe that is known to fail.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Burst pipe.</strong> After a hard freeze, a pipe can split in a wall
        or an attic. Gwinnett and Walton had a lot of these in December 2022.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Storm and lightning damage.</strong> Rain gets in through a roof
        damaged by wind or a falling tree. A lightning strike can start a fire, and
        then there is water from putting it out.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets.</strong> Do not clean these
        up yourself. Call.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>If water is in the house now</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Shut the water off, if you can do it safely. Stay away from wet outlets.</li>
      <li>Take photos of the water, the room, and where it started.</li>
      <li>
        Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets: <CallLink prefix="call" /> or
        <a href="#request">send the form</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Already stopped a small drip? Use the
        <a href="/small-leaks">small-leak steps</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Call your insurance company when you can. If you plan to file a claim, do
      not start cutting out drywall first.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not wait until tomorrow to dry things out. Wet drywall and flooring get
      worse overnight, and mold can start in 24 to 48 hours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Questions from Loganville homeowners</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      <div>
        <dt>
          My sump pump stopped working in a storm and the basement is wet. What do
          I do?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Do not step into standing water in the basement to check the pump. The
          wiring may be live. If you can reach the breaker without standing in
          water, turn off the power to the basement. Take photos of the water line
          and what got wet, and call. Tell us it is a sump pump or outside water,
          since that is handled differently from a pipe leak.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>What does terrace level mean, and why do you ask?</dt>
        <dd>
          A terrace level is a finished level partly below ground, often with a
          door out to the yard. Water from outside collects there, and a leak
          upstairs can run down into it. When you call, tell us if you have one,
          and which rooms are on it. That changes how the job is done.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Does it matter which county I am in?</dt>
        <dd>
          It can. The Gwinnett and Walton line runs through Loganville, and the
          county can change who handles your water and some local rules. It also
          can change which company comes out. Give your street and ZIP, and say
          which county you think you are in. If a company cannot come to your
          address, we will say so.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          Lightning hit my house, there was a fire, and now the first floor is
          soaked. Who do I call?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Call 911 first if there is any fire or smoke. Once the fire department
          is done, the water used to put out the fire is water damage, and we can
          help with that. Smoke and fire cleanup is a different job. Tell us what
          happened when you call so the right company answers. Take photos before
          anything is moved.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Questions about drying time, insurance, or whether you have to hire the
      company that calls are answered on <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> and
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <p>
      <strong>What a job looks like.</strong> A crew checks the house, pulls out
      standing water, dries the structure, and replaces what can't be saved. You
      should be shown moisture readings, not just told it looks dry. How long it
      takes depends on the job. <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Clean water, dirty water, sewage.</strong> A broken supply line is
      not the same as a washer overflow or a sewer backup. Say which one it is on
      the form.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Insurance.</strong> A sudden burst pipe or a failed appliance is often
      covered. A slow leak, outside water, or sewage may not be unless you bought extra
      coverage. Take photos first. Don't tear out drywall to "help." Ask your insurer.
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;" id="request">
    <h2>Request help</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Name, phone, city, ZIP, and what happened. Someone will call you back.
    </p>
    <LeadForm defaultCity="loganville" />
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h2>Nearby</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/grayson">Grayson</a> ·
      <a href="/snellville">Snellville</a> ·
      <a href="/dacula">Dacula</a> ·
      <a href="/winder">Winder</a> ·
      <a href="/gwinnett-county">Gwinnett County</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
